-- title:
--   Twisted averages of pattern functions
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. Let $m\in\mathbb N$.
--
--   1. **Vector twist.** For $\varphi(s,\varepsilon)$,
--   $$
--   \tilde\varphi(s)=\mathbb E_\varepsilon\,\varphi(\varepsilon\oplus s,\varepsilon).
--   $$
--   2. **Matrix twist.** For $\Phi(S,\varepsilon,\delta)$, $\tilde\Phi(S)=\mathbb E_\varepsilon\mathbb E_\delta\,\Phi(\mathrm{row}(\varepsilon)\oplus\mathrm{col}(\delta)\oplus S,\varepsilon,\delta)$.
--
--   These averages turn tuple pairings into normalized scalar functions of sign patterns, as required by the coefficient target.
